WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.177
Transformers can understand and generate language, but they do not automatically have access to every source

00:00:08.177 --> 00:00:11.822
of information we care about.

00:00:11.822 --> 00:00:15.942
A model may have been pretrained before a policy changed.

00:00:15.942 --> 00:00:20.902
It may never have seen an organization’s private PDFs, internal reports, course documents,

00:00:20.902 --> 00:00:24.302
or database records.

00:00:24.302 --> 00:00:30.802
Even if a model has a large context window, sending an entire document collection for every question is

00:00:30.802 --> 00:00:37.182
often inefficient and unfocused. Retrieval-Augmented Generation separates the problem into two stages.

00:00:37.182 --> 00:00:45.392
First, a retrieval system searches external information and selects the passages most relevant to the user’s

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:51.952
question. Second, those passages are placed into the context of a generative model,

00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:55.662
which uses them to produce an answer.

00:00:55.662 --> 00:01:02.672
In Module 7 we will build that pipeline step by step: document loading, chunking, embeddings,

00:01:02.672 --> 00:01:08.957
vector indexes and databases, sparse and dense retrieval, hybrid search, reranking, context construction,

00:01:08.957 --> 00:01:11.440
citations, and evaluation.
